You have two clean options that only use parent CSS — no JS, no child selectors needed.

## Option 1 — `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)` (works best on full-viewport layouts)

```css
.parent {
  position: relative;          /* required for absolute children */
  width: 100vw;                /* use vw so it scales with window */
  height: 100vh;
  overflow: hidden;            /* keeps background full-bleed */
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);  /* centers in both axes */
}
```

**Why it works:** `top:50%` / `left:50%` puts the child at the parent’s midpoint first. Then `translate(-50%, -50%)` shifts the child’s own box origin back half-way, landing it exactly in the middle.

### Things to watch out for
- **Avoid `margin: 0 auto`** on the child — it breaks the centering calculation. Prefer padding or zero margin.
- **Performance with animated children:** add
  ```css
  .child {
    contain-in-size: contain;   /* prevents layout thrash */
    will-change: transform;     /* hints GPU priorization */
  }
  ```

## Option 2 — Flexbox on the parent (simpler to remember)

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;   /* horizontal center */
  align-items: center;       /* vertical center */
  height: 100vh;             /* add width: 100vw if children are wide */
}
```

This works great for fixed-size children, but on dynamic `100vh` viewports the translate method above tends to be more stable and performant.

---

**TL;DR for your case:** stick with **Option 1**. It’s the standard pattern for centered elements inside full-screen containers (`100vh`) and only needs rules on the parent + individual child classes.

